  "title": "2.4. Connected Sets",
  "lang": "en",
  "description": "We now focus on the fact that the Cantor set has gaps at every scale: given any $x\\in C$ and any $r>0$, no matter how small $r$ is, the neighborhood $(x-r,x+r)$ contains some open interval that does not belong to $C$. Thus, if we zoom in around any point of the Cantor set, the set never starts looking like a solid interval. To express the property of a set forming one global piece, we introduce connectedness.",
